WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.832 --> 00:00:01.665
(whooshing)

00:00:01.665 --> 00:00:03.176
(thudding)

00:00:03.176 --> 00:00:04.853
- Good morning, Believe Nation, it's Evan,

00:00:04.853 --> 00:00:06.093
my one word is believe,

00:00:06.093 --> 00:00:08.878
and I believe that you
have a special ability to

00:00:08.878 --> 00:00:11.559
create something amazing
that can change the planet.

00:00:11.559 --> 00:00:13.418
So, to help you on your journey,
today, we're going to learn

00:00:13.418 --> 00:00:16.457
about how to overcome guilt and shame.

00:00:16.457 --> 00:00:18.450
Rule Number Four is my personal favorite,

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:21.258
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:21.258 --> 00:00:24.758
(bright, energetic music)

00:00:33.921 --> 00:00:37.504
(bright, mysterious music)

00:00:39.297 --> 00:00:40.828
- That living guilty doesn't

00:00:40.828 --> 00:00:42.934
do anything productive.

00:00:42.934 --> 00:00:44.838
It doesn't help you to do better,

00:00:44.838 --> 00:00:47.212
it causes you to struggle more.

00:00:47.212 --> 00:00:49.743
Guilt drains you emotionally.

00:00:49.743 --> 00:00:52.140
Physically, it will wear you out.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.955
When we're guilty, we don't pursue dreams,

00:00:54.955 --> 00:00:57.389
we don't believe to overcome challenges,

00:00:57.389 --> 00:00:58.994
we get stuck.

00:00:58.994 --> 00:01:00.480
This is why the enemy

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.967
works overtime in this area.

00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:07.402
He knows guilt will keep
you from your destiny.

00:01:07.402 --> 00:01:10.031
There's nothing he would love any more

00:01:10.031 --> 00:01:13.473
than for you to go through
life against yourself,

00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:16.818
focused on your failures,
feeling unworthy.

00:01:16.818 --> 00:01:20.088
- Shame is far more likely to
cause destructive behaviors

00:01:20.088 --> 00:01:22.320
than it is to cure it.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:23.153
What we know

00:01:23.153 --> 00:01:25.970
is that shame is highly
correlated with addiction,

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:28.553
violence, aggression, bullying.

00:01:30.211 --> 00:01:32.959
Um, it's not helpful to be ashamed,

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:36.091
for this reason, and I think
this is really a core thing

00:01:36.091 --> 00:01:38.206
that we need to get our
heads and hearts around,

00:01:38.206 --> 00:01:39.400
as a culture.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:44.075
Shame corrodes the part of us
that believes we can change,

00:01:44.075 --> 00:01:47.278
so to shame someone into changing,

00:01:47.278 --> 00:01:48.920
is like saying to them,

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.653
you are horrible, and worthless,

00:01:50.653 --> 00:01:52.669
and you're not capable of change.

00:01:52.669 --> 00:01:53.781
Get better.

00:01:53.781 --> 00:01:58.160
(bright, mysterious music)

00:01:58.160 --> 00:01:59.634
- The best way to deal with shame,

00:01:59.634 --> 00:02:01.561
for people, is to find
what you're most proud of.

00:02:01.561 --> 00:02:02.394
You can't...

00:02:02.394 --> 00:02:03.405
You're never going to deal with shame.

00:02:03.405 --> 00:02:05.832
You have to overwhelm it with the good.

00:02:05.832 --> 00:02:06.665
It's kind of like,

00:02:06.665 --> 00:02:07.498
if every day of your life

00:02:07.498 --> 00:02:08.826
you look around and you let it happen.

00:02:08.826 --> 00:02:09.659
News comes in,

00:02:09.659 --> 00:02:11.454
and it's almost always, these
days, something to scare you.

00:02:11.454 --> 00:02:12.768
If it bleeds, it leads,

00:02:12.768 --> 00:02:14.530
they say in the newspaper business, right?

00:02:14.530 --> 00:02:16.098
So, something to jar you.

00:02:16.098 --> 00:02:17.129
If you're going to have your life,

00:02:17.129 --> 00:02:17.962
you have to do what I call

00:02:17.962 --> 00:02:18.973
stack the good.

00:02:18.973 --> 00:02:20.250
You have to flood yourself with the good.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.083
You have to say,

00:02:21.083 --> 00:02:22.266
what am I proud of in my life?

00:02:22.266 --> 00:02:23.572
What am I grateful for?

00:02:23.572 --> 00:02:24.886
And you've got to literally think of

00:02:24.886 --> 00:02:27.149
three or four or five things,
people, experiences...

00:02:27.149 --> 00:02:28.754
It could be something
little you're proud of,

00:02:28.754 --> 00:02:30.442
When you were a kid, something today.

00:02:30.442 --> 00:02:31.509
But, if you do what I call

00:02:31.509 --> 00:02:33.033
an emotional flood,

00:02:33.033 --> 00:02:35.205
when you flood yourself
with all the beautiful,

00:02:35.205 --> 00:02:37.177
proud things, things you're
grateful for in your life,

00:02:37.177 --> 00:02:39.887
you'll find that'll overwhelm
that feeling of shame,

00:02:39.887 --> 00:02:41.246
and the shame feeling will disappear.

00:02:41.246 --> 00:02:43.546
But, it won't disappear by analyzing it.

00:02:43.546 --> 00:02:44.379
- Right, right, right.

00:02:44.379 --> 00:02:45.801
- Then you just get more in your head.

00:02:45.801 --> 00:02:47.070
- No, I like this idea of flooding.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:47.936
- Yeah, you flood it.

00:02:47.936 --> 00:02:50.318
- So, a lot of people
confuse guilt with shame.

00:02:50.318 --> 00:02:51.587
You say they're cousins.

00:02:51.587 --> 00:02:53.476
- I say they're cousins, kissing cousins.

00:02:53.476 --> 00:02:56.351
Shame says there's something
wrong with who I am.

00:02:56.351 --> 00:02:57.501
It's intrinsic.

00:02:57.501 --> 00:02:58.643
I can't do anything about it.

00:02:58.643 --> 00:03:02.421
Guilt says, there's something
wrong with what I've done.

00:03:02.421 --> 00:03:03.254
- [Oprah] Or am doing.

00:03:03.254 --> 00:03:04.437
- Or am doing.

00:03:04.437 --> 00:03:06.543
Yeah, so I feel guilty about it.

00:03:06.543 --> 00:03:08.918
Even people now feel guilty
about what they think.

00:03:08.918 --> 00:03:10.115
You know what they say.

00:03:10.115 --> 00:03:13.430
Both of them, well,
shame is a little deeper.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:16.036
Guilt, however is a wasted emotion.

00:03:16.036 --> 00:03:17.433
- Yeah, that's what I say.

00:03:17.433 --> 00:03:18.814
- It's a wasted emotion,

00:03:18.814 --> 00:03:21.099
because the minute you find that

00:03:21.099 --> 00:03:23.929
there's something wrong,
or out of alignment,

00:03:23.929 --> 00:03:24.884
and you're out of order,

00:03:24.884 --> 00:03:26.340
in what you're thinking or doing,

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:27.408
do something else!

00:03:27.408 --> 00:03:29.260
But we know we cause

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:31.470
hurt, harm, danger, to someone.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:32.303
- [Oprah] Yeah.

00:03:32.303 --> 00:03:33.136
- That will lead us guilt.

00:03:33.136 --> 00:03:35.318
We look at what we did,
and how it hurt somebody.

00:03:35.318 --> 00:03:37.469
- [Oprah] Shouldn't you
feel guilty about that?

00:03:37.469 --> 00:03:40.346
- Well, no, I think you can make amends.

00:03:40.346 --> 00:03:41.179
- [Oprah] Okay.

00:03:41.179 --> 00:03:42.391
- And you can do better next time.

00:03:42.391 --> 00:03:43.466
What does guilt serve?

00:03:43.466 --> 00:03:45.691
It doesn't serve anything
other than the ego.

00:03:45.691 --> 00:03:46.676
- [Oprah] Okay.

00:03:46.676 --> 00:03:47.702
- Which is to take you out of the--

00:03:47.702 --> 00:03:48.686
- So, the acknowledgement,

00:03:48.686 --> 00:03:51.286
that you cause hurt, harm, or danger,

00:03:51.286 --> 00:03:52.354
- Acknowledgment, that's it.

00:03:52.354 --> 00:03:54.247
- Acknowledgement that you
caused that to somebody

00:03:54.247 --> 00:03:57.145
is not the same thing as
feeling guilty about it.

00:03:57.145 --> 00:04:00.750
- No, acknowledge it, ask for forgiveness,

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:01.777
of yourself, first,

00:04:01.777 --> 00:04:03.281
and then of other people, if you can,

00:04:03.281 --> 00:04:05.486
and if you can do
something to make amends,

00:04:05.486 --> 00:04:06.319
- [Oprah] Yes.

00:04:06.319 --> 00:04:07.152
Then, that will

00:04:07.152 --> 00:04:11.671
release you from the
karmic tie to that action.

00:04:11.671 --> 00:04:12.738
- I have interviewed

00:04:12.738 --> 00:04:14.097
over 600 men and women, now,

00:04:14.097 --> 00:04:15.706
across the country about shame,

00:04:15.706 --> 00:04:18.347
and, across the interviews,

00:04:18.347 --> 00:04:21.620
men and women with high
levels of resilience to shame

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.949
share four things in common.

00:04:23.949 --> 00:04:25.426
Um, the first is,

00:04:25.426 --> 00:04:28.089
they physically recognize
when they're in shame,

00:04:28.089 --> 00:04:29.968
and they know what triggered it.

00:04:29.968 --> 00:04:32.221
So, let's say something happens at work,

00:04:32.221 --> 00:04:34.114
I make a mistake, or something happens,

00:04:34.114 --> 00:04:36.589
and that warm wash comes over me.

00:04:36.589 --> 00:04:38.516
You know that feeling, yes, yeah.

00:04:38.516 --> 00:04:39.655
We all know it.

00:04:39.655 --> 00:04:41.221
- [Interviewer] Yeah, yeah.

00:04:41.221 --> 00:04:42.338
- If I can physically say,

00:04:42.338 --> 00:04:45.015
oh my god, I know that this
is shame, I'm in shame,

00:04:45.015 --> 00:04:46.998
it's very helpful, because,

00:04:46.998 --> 00:04:48.849
we are not fit for human consumption

00:04:48.849 --> 00:04:50.118
when we're in shame.

00:04:50.118 --> 00:04:52.967
We need to pull away, and get regrounded,

00:04:52.967 --> 00:04:54.285
take some deep breaths,

00:04:54.285 --> 00:04:56.413
and figure out how to respond.

00:04:56.413 --> 00:04:58.216
One of the things that makes shame worse

00:04:58.216 --> 00:05:02.071
is normally, when we're in it, we act out.

00:05:02.071 --> 00:05:04.407
For example, the mother
that I interviewed,

00:05:04.407 --> 00:05:07.486
she fills her car up, she
goes into the gas station.

00:05:07.486 --> 00:05:09.788
She goes to pay, her
credit card's declined.

00:05:09.788 --> 00:05:11.965
The man shames her in
front of the other patrons,

00:05:11.965 --> 00:05:14.516
makes her leave her wedding
band and her wallet,

00:05:14.516 --> 00:05:16.645
and leave with just an ATM card.

00:05:16.645 --> 00:05:18.031
So, she's filled with shame.

00:05:18.031 --> 00:05:20.576
She gets in the car, she slams the door.

00:05:20.576 --> 00:05:21.477
When she slams the door,

00:05:21.477 --> 00:05:22.310
it wakes up her

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:23.904
three-year-old toddler
who's in the backseat,

00:05:23.904 --> 00:05:25.311
who starts screaming.

00:05:25.311 --> 00:05:26.663
What happens next?

00:05:26.663 --> 00:05:27.821
She starts screaming back,

00:05:27.821 --> 00:05:29.756
shut up, shut up.

00:05:29.756 --> 00:05:31.447
So, now she's in the shame of having this

00:05:31.447 --> 00:05:34.706
credit card declined, being
belittled in front of people,

00:05:34.706 --> 00:05:37.202
and now, having to deal
with the struggle of

00:05:37.202 --> 00:05:38.062
I'm a horrible mom,

00:05:38.062 --> 00:05:39.864
and why am I taking this out on my child?

00:05:39.864 --> 00:05:42.447
So, men and women with
high levels of resilience

00:05:42.447 --> 00:05:44.748
will recognize physically
when they're in shame,

00:05:44.748 --> 00:05:45.892
and what triggered it.

00:05:45.892 --> 00:05:46.725
- Okay.

00:05:46.725 --> 00:05:49.636
- Then, they practice critical awareness.

00:05:49.636 --> 00:05:54.490
How realistic are the
expectations and messages that we

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:56.674
put on ourselves, or
that others put on us,

00:05:56.674 --> 00:05:58.858
that tell us who we're supposed to be.

00:05:58.858 --> 00:05:59.849
The third thing is,

00:05:59.849 --> 00:06:02.331
men and women with high levels
of resilience reach out.

00:06:02.331 --> 00:06:04.314
We've got to share our story.

00:06:04.314 --> 00:06:05.147
- [Interviewer] So they talk about it.

00:06:05.147 --> 00:06:06.893
- They talk about it, you know.

00:06:06.893 --> 00:06:09.382
We breathe through it,
we recognize we're in it,

00:06:09.382 --> 00:06:11.428
and then we call and say,
hey, Patricia, it's me,

00:06:11.428 --> 00:06:12.613
you're not going to believe what happened.

00:06:12.613 --> 00:06:13.446
Can you listen?

00:06:13.446 --> 00:06:14.279
It's hard.

00:06:15.192 --> 00:06:16.752
And then, we speak shame.

00:06:16.752 --> 00:06:18.229
You know, it's...

00:06:18.229 --> 00:06:19.581
I try to explain to people

00:06:19.581 --> 00:06:21.842
the importance of using the word shame.

00:06:21.842 --> 00:06:24.559
And this is not just laypeople.

00:06:24.559 --> 00:06:26.827
This is mental health professionals.

00:06:26.827 --> 00:06:28.810
You know, we've got to use the word.

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:30.162
It's different, if I
were to call you and say,

00:06:30.162 --> 00:06:32.186
uh, Patricia, it's Brene,
I'm so embarrassed.

00:06:32.186 --> 00:06:33.455
That would mean one thing, right?

00:06:33.455 --> 00:06:34.647
But, if I were to call you and say,

00:06:34.647 --> 00:06:35.674
Patricia, it's Brene,

00:06:35.674 --> 00:06:38.239
and I'm really struggling with some shame,

00:06:38.239 --> 00:06:39.272
that's different.

00:06:39.272 --> 00:06:40.471
- [Interviewer] Uh huh, it's
a different conversation.

00:06:40.471 --> 00:06:43.356
- Right, shame cannot
hold on when we name it.

00:06:43.356 --> 00:06:44.189
- [Interviewr] Right.

00:06:44.189 --> 00:06:46.323
So, it's not just, you
know, an academic pet peeve.

00:06:46.323 --> 00:06:48.133
I want everybody to get
the semantics correct.

00:06:48.133 --> 00:06:50.650
Men and women with high
levels of shame resilience

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:53.402
use the word and talk about the emotion.

00:06:53.402 --> 00:06:55.142
- Someone wrote in saying that

00:06:55.142 --> 00:06:58.345
they wake up in the middle
of the night, saying,

00:06:58.345 --> 00:07:00.023
I shouldn't have done that.

00:07:00.023 --> 00:07:01.368
They feel guilty about

00:07:01.368 --> 00:07:03.420
eating that whole chocolate cake.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:05.646
They feel really bad

00:07:05.646 --> 00:07:07.955
about certain career choices,

00:07:07.955 --> 00:07:10.455
about certain clothes choices,

00:07:11.356 --> 00:07:12.439
food choices.

00:07:13.537 --> 00:07:16.537
Water, those kind of choices, right?

00:07:18.265 --> 00:07:21.878
And, they are now turning on themselves.

00:07:21.878 --> 00:07:24.824
They say it's easy to
forgive other people,

00:07:24.824 --> 00:07:26.946
but when it comes to themselves,

00:07:26.946 --> 00:07:28.950
they have a really hard time.

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:30.350
What should they do?

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:32.763
What's helped me along my journey, is,

00:07:32.763 --> 00:07:36.930
♫ If I could turn back the hands of time

00:07:37.987 --> 00:07:38.820
Right?

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:41.200
If I could turn back the hands of time,

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:42.677
ask yourself,

00:07:42.677 --> 00:07:44.594
would I change my past?

00:07:46.705 --> 00:07:49.566
I'll give you five seconds to answer.

00:07:49.566 --> 00:07:50.835
Okay, that's great.

00:07:50.835 --> 00:07:52.596
So, you've said yes.

00:07:52.596 --> 00:07:53.429
(scoffs gently)

00:07:53.429 --> 00:07:54.281
You said yes.

00:07:54.281 --> 00:07:55.800
You would change your past.

00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:58.185
Okay, let me ask you this.

00:07:58.185 --> 00:08:02.352
If I could take away all the
lessons learned from your past,

00:08:03.558 --> 00:08:05.485
would you allow me to?

00:08:05.485 --> 00:08:06.421
Five seconds.

00:08:06.421 --> 00:08:07.371
Okay, great.

00:08:07.371 --> 00:08:08.454
You said, no.

00:08:09.514 --> 00:08:11.649
So, you want the lessons

00:08:11.649 --> 00:08:13.618
but you don't want the experiences.

00:08:13.618 --> 00:08:14.451
Oh boy.

00:08:14.451 --> 00:08:16.451
Can I get a hello there?

00:08:17.724 --> 00:08:18.641
Okay, okay.

00:08:20.928 --> 00:08:23.014
Okay, this is what's helped me.

00:08:23.014 --> 00:08:26.745
To realize there are
lessons learned, right?

00:08:26.745 --> 00:08:29.774
Everything happens for a reason.

00:08:29.774 --> 00:08:30.607
(imitates fist striking)

00:08:30.607 --> 00:08:32.797
The next person that tells me that...

00:08:32.797 --> 00:08:33.630
What happens?

00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:34.523
When people say that,

00:08:34.523 --> 00:08:37.470
sometimes it feels really cringe-worthy.

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:39.599
Right? Really cringe-worthy.

00:08:39.599 --> 00:08:40.923
Right?

00:08:40.923 --> 00:08:43.232
Do you know what happened
to me in my past?

00:08:43.232 --> 00:08:46.539
Everything happens for a reason.

00:08:46.539 --> 00:08:49.254
Can I get a hello there?

00:08:49.254 --> 00:08:50.087
No.

00:08:52.076 --> 00:08:53.601
It really doesn't have to.

00:08:53.601 --> 00:08:56.298
It really doesn't have
to be like that, right?

00:08:56.298 --> 00:08:57.791
That's what we tell ourselves.

00:08:57.791 --> 00:08:59.085
(gentle, mystical music)

00:08:59.085 --> 00:09:01.542
When I realize lessons learned

00:09:01.542 --> 00:09:04.125
was about not judging our past.

00:09:05.868 --> 00:09:09.035
Right? When you don't judge your past,

00:09:10.292 --> 00:09:12.221
it's a lot easier to accept

00:09:12.221 --> 00:09:14.072
what has taken place.

00:09:14.072 --> 00:09:15.739
We are in disbelief.

00:09:16.839 --> 00:09:18.683
I can't believe it happened.

00:09:18.683 --> 00:09:21.408
I can't believe I got a
scratch on that new car,

00:09:21.408 --> 00:09:24.867
on the first day, in the first hour,

00:09:24.867 --> 00:09:26.779
when I was listening to

00:09:26.779 --> 00:09:28.631
♫ Let's party

00:09:28.631 --> 00:09:30.631
Can I get a hello there?

00:09:32.298 --> 00:09:35.155
Not my favorite song, for goodness' sakes.

00:09:35.155 --> 00:09:37.498
It could have been the other crap one.

00:09:37.498 --> 00:09:42.483
But yeah, sometimes your house
gets robbed on your birthday.

00:09:42.483 --> 00:09:45.534
Sometimes, you buy a new dress,

00:09:45.534 --> 00:09:47.784
and then, you trip up, and,

00:09:49.181 --> 00:09:51.518
mud goes everywhere, and,

00:09:51.518 --> 00:09:52.935
now you feel like

00:09:53.854 --> 00:09:55.899
it shouldn't have happened,

00:09:55.899 --> 00:09:56.816
but it did.

00:09:58.104 --> 00:10:00.247
In dealing with self-forgiveness,

00:10:00.247 --> 00:10:02.431
which really is the gateway, the

00:10:02.431 --> 00:10:05.931
passage to becoming your greatest version,

00:10:08.289 --> 00:10:10.900
you've got to lose saying

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:13.375
it shouldn't have happened.

00:10:13.375 --> 00:10:15.708
Instead, say, it did happen,

00:10:17.064 --> 00:10:20.780
and I'm going to change how
I respond to what happened.

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:23.697
- It's hard to defend yourself when

00:10:26.833 --> 00:10:29.502
the murmuring of the
voice of accusation is

00:10:29.502 --> 00:10:31.335
stronger on the inside

00:10:32.179 --> 00:10:35.631
than the voice of all the
critics on the outside.

00:10:35.631 --> 00:10:39.472
Guilt is not just about what
others are saying about you.

00:10:39.472 --> 00:10:43.355
It is what you are saying about yourself.

00:10:43.355 --> 00:10:47.536
It is, it is the condemnation
that comes upon you.

00:10:47.536 --> 00:10:51.675
It is the struggle that
lives down inside of you.

00:10:51.675 --> 00:10:52.611
That you say...

00:10:52.611 --> 00:10:53.845
Have you ever said,

00:10:53.845 --> 00:10:55.512
what was I thinking?

00:10:57.756 --> 00:10:58.954
Have you ever looked back at somebody,

00:10:58.954 --> 00:10:59.787
and you just wonder,

00:10:59.787 --> 00:11:02.498
what was I on, to do that?

00:11:02.498 --> 00:11:04.046
What happened?

00:11:04.046 --> 00:11:07.213
Did somebody put something in my coke?

00:11:08.082 --> 00:11:10.750
What happened, that I went that far,

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:11.917
you may not...

00:11:13.083 --> 00:11:14.272
See,

00:11:14.272 --> 00:11:17.663
this, this, one of the
things that we do with guilt,

00:11:17.663 --> 00:11:21.246
because it is such a
heavy burden to carry.

00:11:24.665 --> 00:11:25.934
It's a heavy burden,

00:11:25.934 --> 00:11:30.101
because it can only exist
in places where you care.

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:33.103
(audience member clapping)

00:11:33.103 --> 00:11:35.440
It can only exist in
places where you care,

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:39.461
so, it becomes a heavy burden to carry.

00:11:39.461 --> 00:11:41.527
So, what we do is

00:11:41.527 --> 00:11:43.444
we change the narrative

00:11:44.585 --> 00:11:46.002
of what happened,

00:11:47.289 --> 00:11:49.722
to make the memory more palatable.

00:11:49.722 --> 00:11:52.305
- [Woman In Audience] Hmm, wow.

00:11:54.087 --> 00:11:57.867
- We change the narrative
of what happened,

00:11:57.867 --> 00:12:01.424
to make the memory more palatable.

00:12:01.424 --> 00:12:04.841
We block out the parts we are ashamed of,

00:12:06.243 --> 00:12:09.826
and we remember how
people responded to us,

00:12:11.739 --> 00:12:15.090
without remembering how we elicited

00:12:15.090 --> 00:12:17.257
or initiated the response.

00:12:19.801 --> 00:12:22.097
Selective amnesia.

00:12:22.097 --> 00:12:22.930
(audience cheering and
clapping moderately)

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:24.201
Until you own what happens,

00:12:24.201 --> 00:12:26.513
until you take responsibility for it,

00:12:26.513 --> 00:12:29.817
until you stop hiding from the guilt...

00:12:29.817 --> 00:12:32.417
See, because you're trying
to tranquilize the guilt...

00:12:32.417 --> 00:12:35.885
You know why your
daughter disrespected you.

00:12:35.885 --> 00:12:38.325
You know why she cursed you out.

00:12:38.325 --> 00:12:40.013
You know what she saw

00:12:40.013 --> 00:12:42.237
about you and the neighbor
across the street,

00:12:42.237 --> 00:12:44.277
but you blocked out what
happened between you

00:12:44.277 --> 00:12:46.573
and the neighbor across the
street, and talked about,

00:12:46.573 --> 00:12:48.957
I ain't gon let that girl disrespect me,

00:12:48.957 --> 00:12:50.861
she just lost her mind.

00:12:50.861 --> 00:12:52.709
When in reality, you...

00:12:52.709 --> 00:12:54.485
Uh-oh, uh-oh, uh-oh,

00:12:54.485 --> 00:12:55.853
you told me to go deep.

00:12:55.853 --> 00:12:57.402
You told me to go deep.

00:12:57.402 --> 00:13:00.097
You know, I'm not thinking
about them haters.

00:13:00.097 --> 00:13:01.905
I'm not a hater, I'm hurt,

00:13:01.905 --> 00:13:04.049
'cause you beat me like a dog,

00:13:04.049 --> 00:13:05.369
and I'm telling the truth.

00:13:05.369 --> 00:13:09.202
That's not a hater, but
you changed the story,

00:13:15.545 --> 00:13:17.457
because the weight of the matter

00:13:17.457 --> 00:13:19.313
might mean you admitting

00:13:19.313 --> 00:13:20.761
my son is acting crazy

00:13:20.761 --> 00:13:22.945
because I wasn't a good father,

00:13:22.945 --> 00:13:24.889
and I really wasn't there for him.

00:13:24.889 --> 00:13:27.233
And, he sought things out of me,

00:13:27.233 --> 00:13:28.753
that I wish I hadn't done,

00:13:28.753 --> 00:13:30.649
but at that stage of my life I didn't.

00:13:30.649 --> 00:13:32.809
You have to stop telling yourself,

00:13:32.809 --> 00:13:34.995
I don't know why that boy acting crazy.

00:13:34.995 --> 00:13:36.243
You do.

00:13:36.243 --> 00:13:38.275
You do know why he's acting crazy,

00:13:38.275 --> 00:13:39.971
and you're the only one who can fix it,

00:13:39.971 --> 00:13:43.083
because you do know what's wrong with him,

00:13:43.083 --> 00:13:46.555
and you can't, you can't
get healed, brother,

00:13:46.555 --> 00:13:47.915
as long as you're telling yourself,

00:13:47.915 --> 00:13:49.699
well, somebody's got to get away with it,

00:13:49.699 --> 00:13:50.891
it's not my fault.

00:13:50.891 --> 00:13:51.724
He could've done...

00:13:51.724 --> 00:13:54.141
You know what the problem is,

00:13:55.211 --> 00:13:56.795
but the weight.

00:13:56.795 --> 00:14:00.962
(audience members jeering lightly)

00:14:01.827 --> 00:14:03.971
The weight of the guilt.

00:14:03.971 --> 00:14:06.638
You know that you cannot heal it

00:14:08.339 --> 00:14:10.547
if you weren't on it.

00:14:10.547 --> 00:14:14.214
Are you angry about
something you could fix?

00:14:18.371 --> 00:14:21.890
Guilt that leads to a change of behavior

00:14:21.890 --> 00:14:24.074
is a powerful thing.

00:14:24.074 --> 00:14:26.138
Guilt that grabs you by the throat,

00:14:26.138 --> 00:14:27.826
and makes you call me on the phone,

00:14:27.826 --> 00:14:30.706
and say, look, I'm sorry,

00:14:30.706 --> 00:14:32.602
is a powerful thing.

00:14:32.602 --> 00:14:33.962
It's a wonderful thing

00:14:33.962 --> 00:14:36.722
for you to be guilty
enough to humble yourself,

00:14:36.722 --> 00:14:39.194
to pull your pride down,

00:14:39.194 --> 00:14:41.034
pull your images down,

00:14:41.034 --> 00:14:43.921
and pull all the lies you
told to yourself down,

00:14:43.921 --> 00:14:45.705
and say, look, I miss you,

00:14:45.705 --> 00:14:47.641
and I'm tired of us fighting like this,

00:14:47.641 --> 00:14:49.617
and whatever I did to hurt you,

00:14:49.617 --> 00:14:51.265
I'm sorry.

00:14:51.265 --> 00:14:53.489
I'm man enough to look you in the eye,

00:14:53.489 --> 00:14:55.097
and say I was wrong,

00:14:55.097 --> 00:14:56.305
and I'm sorry.

00:14:56.305 --> 00:14:58.222
You'll never be healed,

00:14:59.306 --> 00:15:03.473
(audience members hollering and clapping)

00:15:05.605 --> 00:15:06.946
'til you can fix it.

00:15:06.946 --> 00:15:08.878
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:15:08.878 --> 00:15:10.814
I'd love to know, what did
you think of this video?

00:15:10.814 --> 00:15:12.416
What was the rule that

00:15:12.416 --> 00:15:13.536
most applies to you,

00:15:13.536 --> 00:15:16.053
that you're going to
immediately implement somehow

00:15:16.053 --> 00:15:17.703
into your life, or your business.

00:15:17.703 --> 00:15:19.517
Please let me know, I'd
love to hear from you,

00:15:19.517 --> 00:15:21.085
leave it down in the comments below.

00:15:21.085 --> 00:15:21.918
I also want to give a

00:15:21.918 --> 00:15:23.138
quick shout-out to Clark Kegley.

00:15:23.138 --> 00:15:25.438
Clark, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:15:25.438 --> 00:15:28.186
Your One Word, and doing an
interview on your channel.

00:15:28.186 --> 00:15:29.941
I really, really, appreciate the support,

00:15:29.941 --> 00:15:31.800
and I'm so glad that you enjoyed the book.

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:33.383
- How do you find your passion,

00:15:33.383 --> 00:15:35.422
make money doing what you love,

00:15:35.422 --> 00:15:38.916
and stand out in an
ever-growing noisy world?

00:15:38.916 --> 00:15:42.358
You distill everything down into one word.

00:15:42.358 --> 00:15:43.807
- So, thank you guys again for watching.

00:15:43.807 --> 00:15:45.666
I hope you have an amazing day.

00:15:45.666 --> 00:15:46.898
I believe in you,

00:15:46.898 --> 00:15:49.250
and hope you continue
to believe in yourself,

00:15:49.250 --> 00:15:51.565
and whatever your one word is.

00:15:51.565 --> 00:15:52.759
Much love,

00:15:52.759 --> 00:15:53.909
I'll see you soon.

00:15:53.909 --> 00:15:54.992
(whooshing)

00:15:54.992 --> 00:15:56.449
